"Behind These Hazel Eyes" is a [[pop rock]]<ref>{{cite web |title=What Made Kelly Clarkson's Breakaway a Pop Landmark |date=November 26, 2019 |first=Adit |last=Ahmed |url=https://mindequalsblown.net/music/what-made-kelly-clarksons-breakaway-pop-landmark|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191127095136/http://mindequalsblown.net/music/what-made-kelly-clarksons-breakaway-pop-landmark|url-status=usurped|archive-date=November 27, 2019}}</ref><ref name= "Billboard Staff 2025">{{cite magazine|last= Billboard Staff |title= The 100 Best Songs of 2005: Staff Picks|magazine= [[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date= April 15, 2025 |url=https://www.billboard.com/lists/2005-songs-best/|accessdate= June 1, 2025|quote=...“Behind These Hazel Eyes” is like that classic’s opposite-but-equally-powerful twin sister – similar in that they’re both head-banging pieces of post-breakup pop-rock perfection...}}</ref> [[power ballad]] that was written by Clarkson, Max Martin, and Dr. Luke and produced by the latter two.<ref name="MTV"/><ref>{{cite news|url=http://music.yahoo.com/blogs/chart-watch/chart-watch-extra-american-idols-million-sellers.html |title=Chart Watch Extra: American Idol's Million-Sellers |date=May 1, 2005 |access-date=April 30, 2010 |last=Grein |first=Paul |publisher=[[Yahoo! Music]]. Yahoo! |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131224122127/http://music.yahoo.com/blogs/chart-watch/chart-watch-extra-american-idols-million-sellers.html |archive-date=December 24, 2013 }}</ref> Sheet music for the song shows [[common time]] with a moderate [[tempo]] of 90 [[beats per minute]], in the key of [[A major]], with the vocal range spanning over two [[octave]]s from E<sub>3</sub> to F{{sharp}}<sub>5</sub>.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.musicnotes.com/sheetmusic/mtdFPE.asp?ppn=MN0048797|title=Digital sheet music - Kelly Clarkson - Because of You|access-date=February 29, 2012|work=Musicnotes.com|date=February 7, 2005|publisher=[[Alfred Publishing]]}}</ref> The [[bridge (music)|bridge]] was the only part of the song that was written by Dr. Luke and Martin together with Clarkson face to face.<ref name="Olive"/> The song begins with Clarkson wailing "oh oh oh" over a restless percussion.<ref name="blogcritics">{{cite news|url=http://blogcritics.org/music/article/single-review-kelly-clarkson-behind-these/|title=Single Review: Kelly Clarkson, "Behind These Hazel Eyes"|date=August 14, 2005|access-date=January 16, 2012|page=2|last=Avoledo|first=Pam|publisher=[[Blogcritics]]. Technorati, Inc|archive-date=April 14, 2013|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130414100905/http://blogcritics.org/music/article/single-review-kelly-clarkson-behind-these/|url-status=dead}}</ref> In the first verse, the music becomes quiet to focus on Clarkson's vocals, as she wails, "Seems like just yesterday/You were a part of me/I used to stand so tall/I used to be so strong/Your arms around me tight/Everything it felt so right/Unbreakable like nothing could go wrong."<ref name="blogcritics"/> During the chorus, the sound of electronic guitar is dominant as she vocalizes, "Here I am/Once again/I'm torn into pieces/Can't deny it/Can't pretend/Just thought you were the one/Broken up deep inside/But you won't get to see the tears I cry/Behind these hazel eyes."<ref name="blogcritics"/> Gil Kaufman of [[MTV]] noticed that the song "soared on crunchy guitars, driving beats, and anthemic, agitated choruses."<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1562780/kelly-clarkson-lp-darker-but-not-radical-departure.jhtml|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111113063059/http://www.mtv.com/news/articles/1562780/kelly-clarkson-lp-darker-but-not-radical-departure.jhtml|url-status=dead|archive-date=November 13, 2011|title=Kelly Clarkson's My December: Rumors Aside, LP Isn't A Radical Departure|date=June 19, 2007|access-date=March 7, 2012|last=Kaufman |first= Gil|publisher=[[MTV]]. Viacom}}</ref>

Lyrically, the song narrates the story of a failed relationship that initially started off well. Clarkson regrets having allowed herself to be vulnerable to her ex-boyfriend, and she is determined that despite the pain that she feels, he will not get the satisfaction of seeing her cry.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.last.fm/music/Kelly+Clarkson/_/Behind+These+Hazel+Eyes|title=Kelly Clarkson – Behind These Hazel Eyes (3:16) |access-date=March 6, 2012|publisher=[[Last.fm]]. CBS Interactive}}</ref> Michael Paoletta of ''[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]'' praised Clarkson's vocals, writing, "Clarkson simply delivers a loose, tour-de-force vocal that simmers alongside a steroid-charged musical backdrop that is fun, fast, and furious."<ref>{{cite magazine |last= Paoletta |first=Michael |date=August 27, 2005|title= Billboard Picks Music - Essential Reviews |magazine= Billboard |volume= 117|issue=  15|pages=68 |publisher=Prometheus Global Media}}<!--|access-date=February 29, 2012--></ref> Scott Juba of ''The Trade'' praised the production of the song, writing, "The song's strong hook pulls listeners in and involves them in the lyrics without ever becoming gimmicky or manipulative." He also complimented Clarkson's vocal performance, which "oscillates between pain and defiance with near pinpoint accuracy."<ref name="The-trades">{{cite news|url=http://www.the-trades.com/article.php?id=2926|title=Music Review: Kelly Clarkson, "Breakaway"|access-date=February 29, 2012|last=Scott|first=Elizabeth|publisher=The-trades.com. Burlee LLC|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121213184439/http://www.the-trades.com/article.php?id=2926|archive-date=December 13, 2012}}</ref>

sections.text:
{{listen|pos=left|filename=Kelly Clarkson - Behind These Hazel Eyes.ogg|title="Behind These Hazel Eyes" (2004)|description= The 19-second chorus of "Behind These Hazel Eyes", which features the dominant sound of electric guitar|format=Ogg|}}

"Behind These Hazel Eyes" is a pop rock power ballad that was written by Clarkson, Max Martin, and Dr. Luke and produced by the latter two. Sheet music for the song shows common time with a moderate tempo of 90 beats per minute, in the key of A major, with the vocal range spanning over two octaves from E<sub>3</sub> to F{{sharp}}<sub>5</sub>. The bridge was the only part of the song that was written by Dr. Luke and Martin together with Clarkson face to face. The song begins with Clarkson wailing "oh oh oh" over a restless percussion. In the first verse, the music becomes quiet to focus on Clarkson's vocals, as she wails, "Seems like just yesterday/You were a part of me/I used to stand so tall/I used to be so strong/Your arms around me tight/Everything it felt so right/Unbreakable like nothing could go wrong." During the chorus, the sound of electronic guitar is dominant as she vocalizes, "Here I am/Once again/I'm torn into pieces/Can't deny it/Can't pretend/Just thought you were the one/Broken up deep inside/But you won't get to see the tears I cry/Behind these hazel eyes." Gil Kaufman of MTV noticed that the song "soared on crunchy guitars, driving beats, and anthemic, agitated choruses."

Lyrically, the song narrates the story of a failed relationship that initially started off well. Clarkson regrets having allowed herself to be vulnerable to her ex-boyfriend, and she is determined that despite the pain that she feels, he will not get the satisfaction of seeing her cry. Michael Paoletta of Billboard praised Clarkson's vocals, writing, "Clarkson simply delivers a loose, tour-de-force vocal that simmers alongside a steroid-charged musical backdrop that is fun, fast, and furious." Scott Juba of The Trade praised the production of the song, writing, "The song's strong hook pulls listeners in and involves them in the lyrics without ever becoming gimmicky or manipulative." He also complimented Clarkson's vocal performance, which "oscillates between pain and defiance with near pinpoint accuracy."

